<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palazzina di caccia of Stupinigi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Stupinigi Hunting Lodge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palazzina di caccia of Stupinigi is a royal hunting lodge south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra">Filippo Juvarra</a> for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> and begun in 1729. Its star-shaped plan centres on an oval hall under a dome crowned by a bronze stag. Used by the house of Savoy for hunts, festivities and weddings, it is part of the Residences of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1997.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palazzina-di-caccia-of-stupinigi.jpg" alt="Palazzina di caccia of Stupinigi" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paris Orlando · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exterior_of_the_Palazzina_di_caccia_of_Stupinigi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with arcaded streets and squares; Guarino Guarini and later Filippo Juvarra, who designed the Basilica of Superga, the hunting lodge of <b>Stupinigi</b> and the façade of Palazzo Madama, made it one of the architectural capitals of Europe, and the Savoy residences are now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
